null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,true,false,true,false,true,true,false,true]}],"solution":"class WordDictionary:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = {}  # char -> child dict; the key \"$\" marks a complete word\n\n    def addWord(self, word: str) -> None:\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in word:\n            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})\n        node[\"$\"] = True\n\n    def search(self, word: str) -> bool:\n        def dfs(node, i):\n            if i == len(word):\n                return \"$\" in node\n            ch = word[i]\n            if ch == \".\":\n                return any(dfs(child, i + 1) for key, child in node.items() if key != \"$\")\n            return ch in node and dfs(node[ch], i + 1)\n\n        return dfs(self.root, 0)\n","approach":"**A trie where the wildcard branches into every child.** Storing words is exactly a trie insert. Searching is a trie walk, except that when the query character is `.` the walk must try every child of the current node and succeed if any of them leads to a match. That is a depth-first search over `(node, position)` states.\n\n**Why it works:** a trie node corresponds to one prefix of a stored word. The DFS explores precisely the set of stored prefixes consistent with the query so far, and the end-of-word flag at the final node guarantees the match has the same length as the query rather than merely being a prefix.\n\n**Complexity:** a letter costs O(1) per level; a dot costs up to 26 branches, so a query with d dots costs O(26^d * L) in the worst case. With at most 2 dots this is fine, which is why the constraint exists.\n\n**Common mistakes:** returning true when the query is exhausted at any node (must check the word flag, otherwise \"ab\" would match stored \"abc\"); treating the `$` marker as a child when expanding a dot; storing words in a list and regex-matching each query (O(total words) per search, which the 1000-word test punishes).\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** what if the query could contain `*` (zero or more letters) like a glob; how would you support a huge number of dots (group words by length and, for very dotty queries, scan the bucket instead); deleting words; returning all matches rather than a boolean.","complexity":{"time":"O(L) for addWord; O(26^d * L) for search with d dots","space":"O(total characters stored)"},"similar":["implement-trie","word-search-ii","wildcard-matching"],"number":78}